  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nUpdated at 1000 PM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nOverview...\n\nA cool front passing through Central Indiana tonight with rain \nchances decreasing through the evening. This front won't make much\nof a difference in temperatures with highs climbing into the low \nto mid 80s for Sunday. High pressure will keep conditions calm and\nmild through early next week. Rain and thunderstorm chances \nreturn Wednesday night into Thursday as another storm system \napproaches from the west, followed by cooler air on Friday.\n\nTonight Through Sunday Night...\n\nA broad upper-level trough over the Upper Midwest will pivot \neastward into the Great Lakes overnight, driving an attendant \nsurface cold front southeastward across Central Indiana between 03Z \nand 10Z. HREF guidance shows a narrow plume of moisture transport \npreceding the boundary, pushing PWAT values near 1.3 to 1.5 inches. \nDespite limited elevated instability, moderate PVA aloft combined \nwith 30-35 kt of 850mb jet dynamics will support a organized band of \nrain showers and embedded thunderstorms through the evening hours \nwith rain chances gradually dropping ahead of the frontal passage. \nHREF ensemble members maintain peak MLCAPE values below 500 J/kg, \nlimiting severe convective potential, though brief heavy downpours \nand wind gusts up to 30 kt remain possible with the strongest cells.\n\nPost-frontal cold advection aloft will increase through Sunday \nmorning in the 925-850mb layer as the surface trough exits. \nDeepening boundary layer mixing under steep low-level lapse rates \nwill allow momentum transfer down to the surface, resulting in \nsustained northwest winds of 10 to 15 mph with gusts up to 15 to\n20 mph. Lingering boundary layer moisture trapped beneath a 850mb\nsubsidence inversion will maintain a broken stratocumulus deck \nthrough midday Sunday before large-scale anticyclonic flow and dry\nair advection scour out remaining cloud cover. As surface high \npressure builds eastward into the middle Mississippi Valley Sunday\nnight, clearing skies and decoupling surface winds will foster \nefficient radiational cooling, dropping low temperatures into the \nupper 40s to lower 50s across the forecast area.\n\nMonday Through Saturday...\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Ohio Valley on Monday will \nshift off the Mid-Atlantic coast by Tuesday. Mid-level height rises \nacross the Midwest under building ridge flow will maintain strong \nbroad-scale subsidence across Central Indiana. The combination of \ndry atmospheric profiles and gentle southerly return flow on the \nbackside of the surface high will allow high temperatures to \nmoderate from the upper 70s to low 80s on Monday with much warmer \ntemperatures arriving Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nThe synoptic pattern transitions to a more active configuration by \nmid to late week. Both operational GFS and ECMWF, supported by their \nrespective GEFS and EPS ensemble means, depict a deep mid-level \ntrough ejecting out of the Southern Rockies into the Central Plains \non Wednesday. Strong warm advection and an intensifying 45 to 55 kt \n850mb low-level jet will direct a surge of Gulf moisture northward \ninto the region, pushing PWAT values above 1.60 inches by Wednesday \nnight. Enhanced upper-level jet dynamics and increasing PVA ahead of \nthe advancing cold front will support widespread rain showers and \nembedded thunder late Wednesday night into Thursday. While shear \nprofiles appear sufficient for organized convection, the overall \nmagnitude of instability will depend heavily on cloud cover and \ndiurnal timing. The cold front completes its passage by Friday \nmorning, returning the region to northwest flow aloft, post-frontal \nCAA, and drier, cooler conditions heading into the weekend.",
